{"name":"Veranda","hex":"#61a9a5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"veranda","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A gallery, platform, or balcony, usually roofed and often partly enclosed, extending along the outside of a building. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/veranda"}],"entries":[{"title":"Veranda","blocks":[{"text":"A veranda is a roofed, open-air hallway or porch, attached to the outside of a building. A veranda is often partly enclosed by a railing and frequently extends across the front and sides of the structure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/GrandeVerandah.jpg/330px-GrandeVerandah.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veranda"},{"title":"Veranda (magazine)","blocks":[{"text":"Veranda is an American lifestyle magazine with a focus on the home, and has a circulation of 464,357 copies as of 2020. The magazine is one of the Hearst Corporation's shelter magazine titles, alongside Elle Décor and House Beautiful. The magazine's headquarters was originally in Atlanta, GA, but relocated to Hearst's editorial offices in Manhattan circa 2013, and then to Hearst's editorial offices in Birmingham, Alabama at the end of 2018. With each of these relocations came rounds of layoffs."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/70/Veranda_%28magazine%29_interior_design_home_furnishings.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veranda_(magazine)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Harlaxton_House.jpg/500px-Harlaxton_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Harlaxton_House.jpg/1280px-Harlaxton_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig